--TEST--
A `secret` value in an html template's hole is refused where it is written, with no escape and no carrier bypass — `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`, `rule:core-classes/html-template`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A hole is an output position, so `rule:security/secret-sinks-refuse`
// answers it the way it answers every other: escaping fully neutralizes
// injection and does nothing for confidentiality, so an escaped credential is
// still a leaked credential. The refusal is at the hole rather than at the
// sink the template reaches, because that is where it is written.
tainted string $name = "ada";
secret string $token = "s";

// The accepted half first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal: `tainted` is accepted, because the escape
// neutralizes it and the sink never distinguishes the two.
echo html`<p>{$name}</p>`, "\n";

// The refused half. Both hole spellings reach it, since the grammar's two
// halves are one rule.
echo html`<p>{$token}</p>`, "\n";
echo html`<p>$token</p>`, "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0790]: an `html` template cannot print a `secret` value
   --> case.nvs:17:15
%A
error[E0790]: an `html` template cannot print a `secret` value
   --> case.nvs:18:14
%A
